# Per-screen safety tips (11a-SOURCE-SAFETY-TIPS.md), English master.
# Key scheme: tip-<topic>-title (S02b heading), tip-<topic>-n (NORMAL tip, always visible,
# <= 25 words), tip-<topic>-h (HIGHER-RISK detail inside <details>, <= 80 words).
# All tip text is sec:critical (11a TIP-006). Wording rules: honest and bounded (never promise
# anonymity), calm, action first, no unlawful advice, no clickable external links (05 GP-2..GP-7).

## Shared chrome

tip-region = Staying safe on this page
# @class critical
tip-high-summary = If you are at higher risk
tip-more = All safety tips, step by step
tip-page-step = Safety tips
tip-page-h1 = Safety tips for each step
# @class critical
tip-page-intro = These tips explain how people who report are most often found, and what you can do about it. Read the higher-risk parts if any of the higher-risk signs on the Safety Check page apply to you. Opening them is not sent to anyone.

## Device

# @class critical
tip-device-title = Your device
# @class critical
tip-device-n = Use your own computer, not one from work. Work devices can record what you type and which sites you open.
# @class critical
tip-device-h = Use a computer that has never been used for work or joined to a work account, or start it from **Tails** on a new USB stick. Phones are harder to keep private because they record location and app use. If you only have a phone, use Tor Browser for Android at Safest and close it when you finish.

## Network

# @class critical
tip-network-title = Your network
# @class critical
tip-network-n = Don't use a work or school network. Whoever runs a network can see that you use Tor, and when.
# @class critical
tip-network-h = A **bridge** in Tor Browser makes Tor harder to spot, but it does not fully hide it. Use a network that is not linked to your name, and don't use the same one every time. On public Wi-Fi, sit away from cameras and other screens, and leave your phone at home so its location history does not place you there.

## Browser

# @class critical
tip-browser-title = Your browser
# @class critical
tip-browser-n = Use Tor Browser set to Safest. A VPN or a "private window" in another browser does not protect you in the same way.
# @class critical
tip-browser-h = Get Tor Browser only from the Tor Project, over Tor if you can, and check its signature. If a yellow JavaScript warning shows on this page, set the security level to Safest before you go on. When you finish, choose **New Identity** and close the browser.

## Place

# @class critical
tip-place-title = Where you are
# @class critical
tip-place-n = Make sure no one can see your screen. Think about people behind you, windows and cameras.
# @class critical
tip-place-h = Places can be checked against camera footage, card payments and phone location records. Switch off or leave your phone before you set out, not when you arrive. Pay cash, and don't keep going back to the same place. Pick times that fit your normal routine.

## Accounts

# @class critical
tip-accounts-title = Accounts and searches
# @class critical
tip-accounts-n = Don't log in to email, social media or any other account while you use this site.
# @class critical
tip-accounts-h = Logging in links what you do to you. Don't look up the people or the subject of your report from work or personal accounts around the time you report. If you need to look something up, do it in Tor Browser without logging in, and not only right before you send.

## Timing

# @class critical
tip-timing-title = Timing
# @class critical
tip-timing-n = Don't send right after you open, copy or print documents. Systems often record who looked at what, and when.
# @class critical
tip-timing-h = Investigations often start with a list of who opened a file and then look at who did something unusual soon after. Wait days or weeks if you safely can, and don't send right after a meeting or a news story. On the review step you can choose to deliver your report **after a random delay of 1 to 3 days**.

## Files

# @class critical
tip-files-title = Files and copies
# @class critical
tip-files-n = Describe or retype what you know when you can. Original files can carry hidden names, dates and device details.
# @class critical
tip-files-h = A document may be a **unique copy** made for only a few people. Small changes in wording, spacing or hidden marks can show whose copy it was. Send only what many people had, or describe it in your own words. Never send something only you could open.

## Hidden data

# @class critical
tip-metadata-title = Hidden data in files
# @class critical
tip-metadata-n = Photos and files can hide names, places and the device used. File names are replaced by default.
# @class critical
tip-metadata-h = Cleaning does not remove everything. A camera leaves its own pattern in every photo, and the background, a reflection or a user name on a screen can point to you. Office files can keep tracked changes and old versions. Keep originals out of cloud folders and synced phones.

## Printing and screens

# @class critical
tip-print-title = Printing, screenshots and photos of screens
# @class critical
tip-print-n = Don't print documents to send them. Printers keep records, and many add tiny tracking dots to each page.
# @class critical
tip-print-h = A printed page, or a scan of it, can show which printer made it, and print servers log who printed what. A US contractor was identified this way in 2017. Screenshots and photos of a work screen can show your name, your windows or the time.

## Content

# @class critical
tip-writing-title = Details that point to you
# @class critical
tip-writing-n = Write only what is needed. Leave out details that only you, or a few people, would know.
# @class critical
tip-writing-h = For each fact, ask who else knew it, and when. If fewer than five people knew it, leave it out or make it less exact. Meetings you attended, your role and words said only to you can all point to you.

## Style

# @class critical
tip-style-title = Your writing style
# @class critical
tip-style-n = Write in short, plain sentences. Your usual words, spelling and greetings can be matched to emails you wrote at work.
# @class critical
tip-style-h = Computer tools can compare writing across many people. Avoid your favorite phrases, usual mistakes, emojis and sign-offs. Don't copy text from your own work messages.

## AI tools

# @class critical
tip-ai-title = AI tools and translators
# @class critical
tip-ai-n = Don't paste your report into AI tools, translators or grammar checkers. They can keep what you type.
# @class critical
tip-ai-h = Online writing tools often keep text and link it to your account, device or network. Spell-check add-ons may send text too. Type your report here, in Tor Browser. If you write a draft first, do it on a device you control, never a work device.

## Identity choice

# @class critical
tip-mode-title = Sharing who you are
# @class critical
tip-mode-n = Anonymous is the default. Share who you are only if you choose to. You can still send a report without it.
# @class critical
tip-mode-h = In Confidential mode your name is sealed, but the custodians named on this site can open it. If you give contact details, use ones that are not linked to work or to your usual accounts.

## Who you report about

# @class critical
tip-concerns-title = Who you name
# @class critical
tip-concerns-n = Who you name, or tick here, can hint at where you work. Tick only what you need.
# @class critical
tip-concerns-h = If only a few people work with the person you tick, your choice narrows down who you might be. The triage team sees your ticks. Name only the roles you need kept away from your report.

## Passphrase

# @class critical
tip-passphrase-title = Your passphrase
# @class critical
tip-passphrase-n = Keep your passphrase in your head, or on paper hidden at home. Never in work email, notes apps or cloud storage.
# @class critical
tip-passphrase-h = Learn it by heart, then destroy the paper. A password manager is fine only if it does not sync online. Anyone with your passphrase can read your replies and write as you. Never type it into any other site, and the team will never ask for it.

## Return visits

# @class critical
tip-return-title = Checking for replies
# @class critical
tip-return-n = Check for replies every few days at most. Take the same care each time: own device, Tor Browser, no work network.
# @class critical
tip-return-h = The days you visit could be compared with who used Tor on those days. Visit rarely, from different networks, and never right after an event linked to your report. Put everything into one message rather than many.

## Conversation

# @class critical
tip-conversation-title = Keep the conversation here
# @class critical
tip-conversation-n = Reply only here. Don't move to email, phone or chat, even if someone asks you to.
# @class critical
tip-conversation-h = A message asking you to use another channel, or asking for your passphrase, may be fake. Calls, emails and meetings leave records that can be linked to you. If you must meet, get legal advice first.

## After sending

# @class critical
tip-after-title = After you send
# @class critical
tip-after-n = Act as you normally would. Don't tell anyone, including coworkers, and don't hint that you know about a report.
# @class critical
tip-after-h = Don't search for news of your report from work devices or accounts you log in to. People who show unusual interest can be noticed. If you are questioned, you can ask to speak to a lawyer first. Keep notes about your report off work systems.

## Device checks

# @class critical
tip-seizure-title = If your device is checked
# @class critical
tip-seizure-n = Don't keep notes or drafts about your report on any phone or computer that others can check.
# @class critical
tip-seizure-h = A checked device can show Tor Browser, downloads and recent files. **Tails** keeps nothing after shutdown unless you set it to. Don't destroy anything you may be legally required to keep. Get legal advice if you are under investigation.

## Leaving

# @class critical
tip-leave-title = When you finish
# @class critical
tip-leave-n = When you finish, choose New Identity in Tor Browser, then close it.
# @class critical
tip-leave-h = Also check your Downloads folder and any notes you made. On a shared computer, make sure no one sees Tor Browser open and that it is not set to start on its own.

## Errors and outages

# @class critical
tip-errors-title = If something goes wrong
# @class critical
tip-errors-n = If something goes wrong, don't retry many times in a row. Come back later, with the same care.
# @class critical
tip-errors-h = Many attempts in a short time can stand out on a network. Close Tor Browser and try again another day if you need to. Never switch to a work device, a normal browser or a non-Tor link to make it work.

## Limits

# @class critical
tip-limits-title = What this site cannot do
# @class critical
tip-limits-n = No tool can hide you completely. This site cannot hide what your files and words reveal, or what a watched device sees.
# @class tier0
tip-limits-h = On this website version, someone who takes over or legally forces the server while you use it could capture what you type and your passphrase. For the highest risk, use the Candor Source App, downloaded over Tor, ideally on Tails.
